 only clean if it's  produced  from electricity that in and of itself  does not emit co2 and there are  different ways to make hydrogen  and this is kind of the biggest  bottleneck right now which is how do you  produce hydrogen  and do it in a way that does not emit  greenhouse gas emissions  so in 10 years or in the future i hope  to see  a a really a big push towards a change  in transition in our energy  uh systems where we have renewable  energy  capacity like solar and wind and  hydroelectricity  abound and then we have renewable two  hydrogen technologies where we can  then capture that renewable electricity  and store it into a hydrogen fuel  using these new technologies and then at  that point we can have distributed  hydrogen  distributed energy why is this important  it means that  we can reduce all of the emissions that  you would normally see  from large-scale transportation like  air flight or freight or trains or  trucks  and this could be potentially extremely  exciting for  for helping us get to our net zero goals  well that sounds like a great  vision but i guess there are some  obstacles as well to reach this goal  right  absolutely make no mistake this is going  to require a massive  amount of investment in infrastructure  and  on top of that they're a massive  investment in research and development  and  science quite frankly the international  energy agency  released this year its energy technology  perspectives report  for 2020. they analyzed over 800  technology pathways  and they found for us to reach net zero  by 2050  more almost half of the technologies  needed to reach this goal  are not yet commercial which means they  are either  in the lab or at a prototype  or even at a pilot demonstration so  there there needs to be a massive amount  of investment not only  in actually scaling up the  infrastructure for transportation of  this clean hydrogen  for the production of this clean  hydrogen and legalization  but also on the underlying technologies  to help us get there  it's going to take a lot of work and one  of the biggest issues right now  is getting the cost down to move produce  and use hydrogen at the heart of this is  new technologies and catalysts that can  convert  energy into hydrogen or membranes or  systems  so there's there's a lot of work still  to be done but  it's a it's a very exciting time in the  field yeah i guess so and we are looking  forward to  learn more from you in one of the  sessions at the world science summit  thank you so much it was a pleasure  talking to you mr luna  thank you so much
 

